Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 10:48:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08017/11362] [outer-loop] The ARC bottleneck is world-model
 induction, not the gate that rejects it -- three levers ruled out with
 evidence

Banked levels on held-out games has been flat at 3/3 across an inducer migration that
made the generator ~6x better. That reads as "the lever we tried was weak." It is not:
it is the result every lever in this area will produce until the finding below moves.

THE CHAIN, each link measured.

1. Retention acts downstream of admission -- it decides WHICH refinement round's engine
   the store holds, which can only matter if the stored engine reaches the policy.

2. Multi-round refinement and reaching the policy are PERFECTLY anti-correlated across
   the 13 cells of the 2026-07-28 run: 0 cells have both, 11 are multi-round without
   reaching, 2 reach with a single round. Structural, not incidental -- a second round
   runs BECAUSE round 1 failed admission; if the later rounds fail too nothing is
   accepted, and if round 1 passes refinement stops and retention is a definitional
   no-op. Retention's precondition and its opportunity are near-mutually-exclusive.

3. Admission is where it dies: 12 of 17 attempts had NO round accepted, 9 of them on
   `world_model_accuracy_below_threshold`.

4. Relaxing the threshold does NOT help. `heldout_accuracy` over 45 rounds is
   degenerate-bimodal -- 32 exactly 0.0, 9 exactly 1.0, 4 in between -- so thresholds
   from the shipped 1.0 down to 0.6 admit the IDENTICAL 9 rounds. No continuum exists.

5. Nor does the graded metric. `CARNOT_ARC_TRUST_METRIC=cell_recall` already exists;
   computed for the real induced engines from exp6012's recorded correct/true changed
   cells over 11 games x 3 seeds: median 0.0033, max 0.0870. A graded gate at 0.90,
   0.75, 0.50, 0.25 or even 0.10 admits 0/33 in every case.
   (Recorded honestly: my first attempt at this was a NON-TEST -- exp6012 calls
   WorldModelVerifier(...).score() directly and never reaches `_score_accuracy`, the
   only place that reads the env var, so setting it changed nothing. The number above
   is computed from the recorded per-row cell counts instead.)

6. The gate is not broken: in the same matrix a correct hand-written `base` engine is
   admitted 31/33 and `identity` 0/33. It discriminates exactly as designed.

WHAT THE ENGINES ARE DOING. Exact-match accuracy sits at intermediate values (0.125-0.6)
while cell recall on the SAME rows is ~0.003. That pair has one explanation: the engines
predict NO-OP frames correctly -- most cells do not change, so copying the input scores
well -- and get essentially every CHANGED cell wrong. On the cells that carry the
dynamics the induced world model behaves like the identity engine, which the gate
correctly rejects. The failure is not "slightly imperfect model, overly strict gate"; it
is "the model has not learned the dynamics, and the gate is correctly saying so."

The agent's own source already suspected this (arc_competition_agent.py:5751 records the
gate "is rarely met (0/6 rounds ... on g50t)"). This supplies the reason and rules out
the three obvious fixes.

CAVEAT, stated not buried: the cell-recall figures come from arc_e3_origin_fixtures,
whose engines predate retention and may include last-write-wins-corrupted stores, which
weakens point 5 as a claim about freshly-induced engines. Points 3 and 4 are measured on
fresh live inductions and are unaffected.

Logged as an open verifier gap (HIGH) because it reframes the ARC roadmap: admission-side
and retention-side proposals are downstream of this and should wait. Cheapest next step is
CPU-only over existing fixtures -- diff a correct `base` engine against the induced one on
changed-cell predictions and characterise the error class.

